var e={version:1,language:`german`,chapter:3,title:`How Are You?`,drivablePrefix:5,sourceHash:`fnv1a64:ea1e994c6b6448e0`,lessons:[{id:`GE-C03-danke`,sequence:105,title:`danke — "thank you," which is really "I think of you"`,headword:`danke`,romanization:`danke`,gloss:`thank you — which is really "I think of you"`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:8b0ded35a2fc98ca`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You can introduce yourself in German. Next comes how are you? — and every answer to it leans on one word. It hides a secret that English shares and hides too: to thank is to think.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: danke`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`danke — "thank you."`}]},{index:2,type:`pronunciation`,title:`Sounds you'll need`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`vowel-a-german — DAHN-kuh: a pure open ah, the vowel of English father, and a final -e that sinks to a soft uh rather than disappearing. Stress the front: DAHN-kuh.`}]},{index:3,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`danke comes from denken, "to think." The old idea: to thank someone is to keep them in mind — "I think of you," "you are in my thoughts." German Dank ("thanks") and Gedanke ("a thought") are the same root wearing two coats.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`And here is the payoff for an English speaker: English "thank" and "think" are the same word too. Old English þancian ("thank") and þencan ("think") split from one root — to thank was to hold in thought. So danke is not foreign at all; it is English thank with the think still visible inside it.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Three different pictures of gratitude, worth holding side by side:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Language`,`"Thanks"`,`Literal idea`],columns:3,rowCount:3,utterances:[`Language: German. "Thanks": danke. Literal idea: "I think of you".`,`Language: French. "Thanks": merci. Literal idea: a reward, a mercy.`,`Language: Spanish. "Thanks": gracias. Literal idea: grace, favour.`]}]},{index:4,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"danke" — DAHN-kuh, stress the front`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "danke" — *DAHN-kuh*, stress the front]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"danke" then "denken" — thanks, and the thinking inside it`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "danke" then "denken" — thanks, and the thinking inside it]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`English "thank" then "think" — one root, two coats`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: English "thank" then "think" — one root, two coats]`}]},{index:5,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What everyday verb is danke built from? (Denken, "to think.") What two English words share that same split? (Thank and think.) Where does the stress fall? (On the front: DAHN-kuh.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-schoen`,sequence:107,title:`schön — "beautiful," and your first umlaut with rounded lips`,headword:`schön`,romanization:`schön`,gloss:`beautiful, nice — and the word that upgrades a thank-you`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:2616b504288049f5`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Danke on its own is complete and polite. But German has a one-word upgrade, and the upgrading word is worth having on its own — it is one of the commonest adjectives in the language.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: schön`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`schön — "beautiful, lovely, nice."`}]},{index:2,type:`pronunciation`,title:`Sounds you'll need`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`umlaut-oe — ö is made by saying ay and then rounding your lips as if to whistle, without moving your tongue. English has no such vowel, so build it from two things you can already do: hold the e of bed, then push your lips forward into an o shape. That is ö.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`sch-sh — sch is simply English sh. So schön is shurn — with no r actually sounded, the way a Southern English speaker says fern.`}]},{index:3,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`schön is English sheen. Both come from a Germanic root meaning bright, shining — so the oldest sense of schön is not "pretty" but "gleaming," and English kept the shine while German took the beauty.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Now snap it onto the word you already have:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`danke schön — "thank you nicely" becomes "thank you very much."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Literally you are thanking someone beautifully. English has no equivalent move; the nearest is the tone of voice in "thanks so much."`}]},{index:4,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"ay" then round your lips — "ö"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "ay" then round your lips — "ö"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"schön" (shurn)`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "schön" (*shurn*)]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"danke schön" — thank you very much`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "danke schön" — thank you very much]`}]},{index:5,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does schön mean, and which English word is it? ("Beautiful"; sheen.) How do you make ö? (Say ay, then round the lips.) Upgrade a thank-you. (Danke schön.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-vielen-dank`,sequence:109,title:`vielen Dank — "many thanks," with the noun stepping forward`,headword:`vielen Dank`,romanization:`vielen Dank`,gloss:`many thanks — the noun behind danke, and the warmest everyday thank-you`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:28cbc995efa43e62`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Danke is a verb form — "I thank." But English also has a noun, thanks, and so does German. Meet it, and a second upgrade opens up.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: der Dank`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`der Dank — "the thanks, the gratitude" (masculine).`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Capital D, because every German noun is capitalised — the rule from Tag. Said dahnk, with the final k crisp.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`viel means "much," and it is English full — a full measure of something. Put it in front of Dank and you get the phrase Germans reach for when danke alone feels too thin:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`vielen Dank — "many thanks."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`The -en on vielen is the same kind of adjective ending you met on guten Tag: German adjectives dress to match their noun. You do not have to build it yet — learn vielen Dank whole, the way you learned guten Tag whole.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`So the family now reads: danke (I thank), der Dank (the thanks), Gedanke (a thought) — all from denken, "to think."`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"der Dank" — the noun, capital D`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "der Dank" — the noun, capital D]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"vielen Dank" — many thanks`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "vielen Dank" — many thanks]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the family — "danke", "Dank", "denken"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the family — "danke", "Dank", "denken"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What is the noun behind danke? (Der Dank.) What English word is viel? (Full — a full measure.) Say "many thanks." (Vielen Dank.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-bitte`,sequence:110,title:`bitte — the Swiss-army word: "please" and "you're welcome"`,headword:`bitte`,romanization:`bitte`,gloss:`please — and also "you're welcome," "here you go," and "pardon?"`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:09993d9c7068fdb5`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Every danke wants an answer. German's answer is a word that also means "please" — and three other things besides, all from one idea.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: bitte`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`bitte — "please," and "you're welcome."`}]},{index:2,type:`pronunciation`,title:`Sounds you'll need`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`i-short — BIT-tuh: a short crisp i as in English bit, a clearly doubled t, and the soft -uh ending you already met on danke.`}]},{index:3,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`bitte is the asking-form of bitten, "to ask, to request, to pray." When you say bitte you are, at root, making a request — "I ask this of you."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`That verb is a close cousin of two English words:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`bid — as in "I bid you welcome," "do as you are bidden": to ask or command.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`bead — astonishingly, yes. A bead was originally a prayer. People counted their prayers (Old English gebed) on a string, and the name of the prayer slid onto the little ball it was counted on. A rosary bead is a frozen request — exactly what bitte still is.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`That single idea, I ask, is what lets one word cover so much ground:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`You say bitte when…`,`It means`,`Why it is all "I ask"`],columns:3,rowCount:4,utterances:[`You say bitte when…: making a request. It means: please. Why it is all "I ask": "I ask you…"`,`You say bitte when…: answering danke. It means: you're welcome. Why it is all "I ask": "I ask nothing back".`,`You say bitte when…: handing something over. It means: here you go. Why it is all "I ask": "I offer, at your request".`,`You say bitte when…: you did not hear. It means: pardon? Why it is all "I ask": "I ask you to repeat".`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Tone does the sorting. The word never changes.`}]},{index:4,type:`culture-pragmatics`,title:`Why it's said this way`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`The courtesy loop closes every small favour in German: Danke. — Bitte. It is the twin of Spanish de nada and French de rien, and leaving off the bitte sounds curt in a way English "no problem" does not.`}]},{index:5,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"bitte" — BIT-tuh, doubled t`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "bitte" — *BIT-tuh*, doubled *t*]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the loop — "danke" … "bitte"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the loop — "danke" … "bitte"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"bitte" as please, then as you're welcome — same word, new tone`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "bitte" as *please*, then as *you're welcome* — same word, new tone]`}]},{index:6,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What verb is bitte from? (Bitten, "to ask, to pray.") What two English words are its cousins? (Bid, and bead — a bead was a prayer.) Name two jobs bitte does. (Please; you're welcome; here you go; pardon?) What answers danke? (Bitte.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-gehen`,sequence:115,title:`gehen — "to go," and it is just English go`,headword:`gehen`,romanization:`gehen`,gloss:`to go — and the verb German uses for "how are you?"`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:100d0c8041c87a1c`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`German asks "how are you?" with a verb of motion. You already own that verb without having studied it, because it is English go.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: gehen`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`gehen — "to go." The form you will need most is geht, "goes."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`That -t is the ending you already met on heißt: third person, one person, one t.`}]},{index:2,type:`pronunciation`,title:`Sounds you'll need`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`h-silent-lengthening — in gehen the h is not breathed. It is a silent letter whose whole job is to stretch the vowel before it: GAY-en, not ge-HEN. So geht is simply gayt.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`This is worth banking now, because German uses the trick everywhere — sehr, Ihnen, Jahr all hide a lengthening h.`}]},{index:3,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`gehen and English go descend from the same Germanic verb — no Latin detour, no false friend, a straight cognate. When you say geht, you are saying goes.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`And that is the whole idea behind German's "how are you?": wellbeing is not something you are, it is something that goes.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Language`,`The verb it asks with`,`The picture`],columns:3,rowCount:3,utterances:[`Language: German. The verb it asks with: gehen, "to go". The picture: how does it go?`,`Language: French. The verb it asks with: aller, "to go". The picture: how does it go?`,`Language: Spanish. The verb it asks with: estar, "to stand". The picture: how are you standing?`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Two of the three put wellbeing in motion; Spanish puts it on its feet.`}]},{index:4,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"gehen" (GAY-en) — the h only stretches`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "gehen" (*GAY-en*) — the *h* only stretches]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"geht" (gayt) — "goes"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "geht" (*gayt*) — "goes"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`German gehen and English go — the same word`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: German *gehen* and English *go* — the same word]`}]},{index:5,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What English word is gehen? (Go — a straight cognate.) What does the h do in gehen? (Nothing but lengthen the vowel.) Which two of the three languages ask "how are you?" with "to go"? (German and French; Spanish uses "to stand.")`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-es`,sequence:116,title:`es — "it," the small word German leans on`,headword:`es`,romanization:`es`,gloss:`it — the placeholder German puts in front of a state`,script:`latin`,modality:`sight`,derivedModality:`sight`,modalityReasons:[`sight-cue`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:81b0d36ae99f80e0`,notice:{modality:`sight`,needs:[`your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down`],waitUntilStopped:[],text:`Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped.`},blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You have the verb: geht, "goes." But goes what? German needs a subject, and for a state — the weather, your day — it reaches for one tiny word.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: es`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`es — "it."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said ess: a short clean e, the vowel of English bet, and a crisp s.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`es is English it. The two look unalike only because of the same swap you already met in das / that: where English keeps a t, German often shows an s. Line them up and the pattern is one column wide:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`English`,`German`],columns:2,rowCount:3,utterances:[`English: that. German: das.`,`English: it. German: es.`,`English: what. German: was.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Same word, one consonant apart — and das, the neuter "the," is es's close relative, which is why both point at things rather than people.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"es" (ess) — "it"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "es" (*ess*) — "it"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"it" then "es"; "that" then "das" — the same t/s swap, twice`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "it" then "es"; "that" then "das" — the same t/s swap, twice]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"es geht" — "it goes"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "es geht" — "it goes"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What is es? ("It.") Which English consonant turns up as s in German? (T — it/es, that/das, what/was.) Say "it goes." (Es geht.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-mir`,sequence:117,title:`mir — "to me," the third coat that ich wears`,headword:`mir`,romanization:`mir`,gloss:`to me, for me — the form German uses for the person a thing happens to`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:e184f543bb924d7b`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You have ich ("I," the doer) and mich ("me," the done-to). German keeps a third form for a different job — and English keeps the same job without a special word for it.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: mir`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`mir — "to me, for me."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said meer: a long ee, and a soft German r at the end that is barely more than a vowel — closer to mee-uh than to an English meer.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`German marks the person something happens to with a form of its own. It is called the dative — the "to-whom" form.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Job`,`German`,`English`],columns:3,rowCount:3,utterances:[`Job: the doer. German: ich. English: I.`,`Job: the done-to. German: mich. English: me.`,`Job: the to-whom. German: mir. English: to me.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`English merged the last two into one word, me, and now signals the difference by word order instead: "she gave me the book" — me is the to-whom there, even though it looks identical to the done-to me in "she saw me." German never merged them, so it can put the to-whom anywhere and still be clear.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`That is why wellbeing works the way it does: something goes, and it goes to a person. The person is in the dative.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the three coats — "ich", "mich", "mir"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the three coats — "ich", "mich", "mir"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"mir" (meer) — "to me"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "mir" (*meer*) — "to me"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"es geht mir" — "it goes to me"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "es geht mir" — "it goes to me"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does mir mean? ("To me, for me.") Which of ich / mich / mir is the to-whom form? (Mir.) How does English show that job instead? (By word order — "gave me the book.")`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-dir`,sequence:119,title:`dir — "to you," and English used to have it too`,headword:`dir`,romanization:`dir`,gloss:`to you — the informal partner of mir`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:3316d8b4c9a645c8`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Mir is "to me." To ask how someone else is doing, you need the same form pointed the other way.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: dir`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`dir — "to you," the to-whom form of du.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said deer, with the same soft trailing r as mir. The two rhyme, which is the easiest way to remember they are a pair.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`dir is English thee — the old to-you form, from the same Germanic root, with the th hardened to d exactly as das answers that.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`English has not lost it entirely. It is fossilised in old phrases: "I give thee thanks," "woe is thee." Those are datives — thee was never the doer, always the to-whom, which is precisely dir's job.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`So the pattern completes itself:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`doer`,`to-whom`],columns:2,rowCount:2,utterances:[`doer: ich. to-whom: mir.`,`doer: du. to-whom: dir.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Remember: dir belongs to du, so it is for friends and family. A stranger gets a different word, and that is the next lesson.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"mir" then "dir" — they rhyme`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "mir" then "dir" — they rhyme]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"du" then "dir" — doer, then to-whom`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "du" then "dir" — doer, then to-whom]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"dir" and English "thee" — the same word`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "dir" and English "thee" — the same word]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does dir mean? ("To you.") Which English word is it? (Thee.) Whom do you say dir to? (Someone you call du — a friend or family.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-wie-geht-es`,sequence:120,title:`Wie geht es dir? — "how are you?"`,headword:`Wie geht es dir?`,romanization:`Wie geht es dir?`,gloss:`how are you? — informal, and every piece already yours`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:ff3ccf944f886e14`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Nothing new is coming. You have all four words — wie, geht, es, dir. This lesson only puts them in order.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: Wie geht es dir?`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Wie geht es dir? — "How are you?"`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Word for word: "How goes it to you?"`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`German`,`English`],columns:2,rowCount:4,utterances:[`wie means how.`,`geht means goes.`,`es means it.`,`dir means to you.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said vee gayt es deer — remember German w says v, and ie is a long ee.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Every piece has been earned: wie from asking someone's name, geht from gehen, es and dir from the last two lessons. This is what the book has been building toward — a real question assembled from parts, not memorised whole.`}]},{index:2,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: Wie geht's?`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`In speech, geht es crushes together into geht's:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Wie geht's? — the form you will actually hear.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`It drops the dir entirely, which makes it neutral: no need to decide between friend and stranger. When in doubt, this is the safe one.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`And you can already answer it:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Wie geht's? — Danke, gut!`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Wie geht es dir?" — vee gayt es deer`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Wie geht es dir?" — *vee gayt es deer*]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the four pieces — "wie", "geht", "es", "dir"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the four pieces — "wie", "geht", "es", "dir"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the short form — "Wie geht's?"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the short form — "Wie geht's?"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the whole exchange — "Wie geht's?" … "Danke, gut!"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the whole exchange — "Wie geht's?" … "Danke, gut!"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does Wie geht es dir? say word for word? ("How goes it to you?") What contracts into geht's? (Geht es.) Why is Wie geht's? the safe form? (It drops dir, so it needs no friend-or-stranger choice.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-ihnen`,sequence:122,title:`Ihnen — "to you," said at a respectful distance`,headword:`Ihnen`,romanization:`Ihnen`,gloss:`to you, formally — the capitalised to-whom that goes with Sie`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:9b8ca04839f34389`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Dir is for a friend. But you already know German splits its "you" in two, and the polite half needs its own to-whom form.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: Ihnen`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Ihnen — "to you," formal.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said EE-nen: the h is silent and merely stretches the i into a long ee, the same trick as in gehen. Stress the front.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Ihnen is, underneath, "to them." German's polite "you" is the third-person plural borrowed wholesale: rather than address you directly, the language speaks of you as though you were a group — distance as respect. Sie is "they" used as "you"; Ihnen is "to them" used as "to you."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`The capital letter is doing real work. Written small, ihnen means "to them" about actual other people. Written Ihnen, it is aimed at the person you are speaking to. Same sound, and only the page can tell them apart.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`to whom`,`informal`,`formal`],columns:3,rowCount:1,utterances:[`to whom: you. informal: dir. formal: Ihnen.`]}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Ihnen" (EE-nen) — long ee, silent h`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Ihnen" (*EE-nen*) — long *ee*, silent *h*]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"dir" then "Ihnen" — friend, then stranger`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "dir" then "Ihnen" — friend, then stranger]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Sie" and "Ihnen" — the polite pair`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Sie" and "Ihnen" — the polite pair]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does Ihnen mean? ("To you," formally.) What does it literally say? ("To them.") What does the capital I tell you? (It is aimed at the listener, not at other people.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-wie-geht-register`,sequence:125,title:`Wie geht es Ihnen? — the same question, at a distance`,headword:`Wie geht es Ihnen?`,romanization:`Wie geht es Ihnen?`,gloss:`the formal "how are you?" — one word changes, the metaphor does not`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:f8641e981deede47`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You have the question for a friend, and you have the formal to-whom word. Putting them together needs no new grammar at all — just a swap.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: Wie geht es Ihnen?`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Wie geht es Ihnen? — "How are you?", to a stranger or an elder.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Listener`,`Question`],columns:2,rowCount:2,utterances:[`Listener: friend, family. Question: Wie geht es dir?`,`Listener: stranger, elder. Question: Wie geht es Ihnen?`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Three of the four words never move. Only the last one changes.`}]},{index:2,type:`grammar`,title:`Grammar Lens: politeness lives in the pronoun`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`This is a rule worth generalising: German carries politeness in the pronoun, not in the sentence. English reaches for extra words to be polite — "I was wondering whether you might…" — and stacks them in front. German usually leaves the sentence exactly as it is and changes who you are calling the person.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You saw the same move with du and Sie. Here it reaches the dative: dir becomes Ihnen, and the politeness is done.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`And when you would rather not choose at all, the contraction sidesteps it: Wie geht's? names no listener, so it commits to nothing.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`to a friend — "Wie geht es dir?"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: to a friend — "Wie geht es dir?"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`to a stranger — "Wie geht es Ihnen?"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: to a stranger — "Wie geht es Ihnen?"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the pair back to back, hearing only the last word change`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the pair back to back, hearing only the last word change]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the neutral escape — "Wie geht's?"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the neutral escape — "Wie geht's?"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What is the formal "how are you?" (Wie geht es Ihnen?) How many words change from the informal? (One — dir to Ihnen.) Where does German keep its politeness? (In the pronoun, not in extra words.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-sehr`,sequence:127,title:`sehr — "very," and it used to mean "sorely"`,headword:`sehr`,romanization:`sehr`,gloss:`very — the intensifier that turns gut into a real answer`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:8d81616857d0982a`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Gut answers "how are you?" perfectly well. One small word in front of it turns a polite answer into a warm one.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: sehr`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`sehr — "very."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said zair: German s before a vowel buzzes like English z, the h is silent and stretches the vowel, and the r softens almost to ah.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Sehr gut! — "Very well!"`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`sehr is English sore. In Middle High German it meant painfully, grievously — and intensifiers very often start life as words for pain, then fade into pure degree. English did exactly the same thing and then half forgot: "sorely missed," "sore afraid" both mean very, not painful.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`So sehr gut is, at the root, "sorely good" — and English very went the same road from a different start, having once meant truly (French vrai).`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"sehr" (zair) — buzzing z, silent h`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "sehr" (*zair*) — buzzing *z*, silent *h*]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"sehr gut" — very well`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "sehr gut" — very well]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"danke sehr" — thanks very much`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "danke sehr" — thanks very much]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does sehr mean? ("Very.") Which English word is it? (Sore — as in "sorely missed.") Answer warmly. (Sehr gut!)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-nicht`,sequence:128,title:`nicht — "not," the same word as English naught`,headword:`nicht`,romanization:`nicht`,gloss:`not — German's plain negator`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:098c03058fa7f0b6`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Not every day is a good one. To say so, you need the one word that turns any German sentence around.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: nicht`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`nicht — "not."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said nikht: the ch is the soft ich-sound from ich and mich, not the throat-scrape of Nacht. The vowel before it is short.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`nicht and English not are one word. Both come from a Germanic phrase meaning no thing at all — literally "not a whit." English shows the longer form still: naught (and nought, the zero) is the undressed version, and not is what happens when centuries of fast speech wear it down.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`So the pair lines up with the ch rule you already have:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`English`,`German`],columns:2,rowCount:2,utterances:[`English: naught. German: nicht.`,`English: night. German: Nacht.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`That English gh is a fossil: it once was the German ch sound, and English stopped pronouncing it while keeping the letters.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Now the answer ladder reaches downward:`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Nicht so gut. — "Not so good."`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"nicht" (nikht) — soft ch`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "nicht" (*nikht*) — soft *ch*]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"naught" then "nicht"; "night" then "Nacht"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "naught" then "nicht"; "night" then "Nacht"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"nicht so gut" — not so good`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "nicht so gut" — not so good]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does nicht mean? ("Not.") Which English word is it, undressed? (Naught.) What does English gh used to be? (The German ch sound.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-so-lala`,sequence:129,title:`so lala — "so-so," and the tune carries it`,headword:`so lala`,romanization:`so lala`,gloss:`so-so — the sing-song middle of the answer ladder`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:0af7c93bd89c0106`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`You can now say gut and nicht so gut. Between them sits the honest middle, and German fills it with a word that means nothing whatsoever.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: so lala`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`so lala — "so-so, middling, could be worse."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Said zo LAH-lah, with the voice rising and falling across the lala — a shrug set to music. Say it flat and it lands wrong; the tune is most of the meaning.`}]},{index:2,type:`etymology`,title:`The word, taken apart`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`There is nothing to take apart, and that is the point. so is English so. lala is not a word at all — it is the same lalala you hum when you do not know the lyrics, pressed into service as a meaning.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`This is not a German quirk. Reach for "so-so" in almost any language and you find a small repeated noise doing the work, because a shrug is easier to sing than to say.`}]},{index:3,type:`culture-pragmatics`,title:`Why it's said this way`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`The shrug collection, four deep across the books in this series:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Language`,`The shrug`,`Literally`],columns:3,rowCount:4,utterances:[`Language: German. The shrug: so lala. Literally: "so la-la".`,`Language: French. The shrug: comme ci, comme ça. Literally: "like this, like that".`,`Language: Spanish. The shrug: más o menos. Literally: "more or less".`,`Language: Italian. The shrug: così così. Literally: "so so".`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Three of the four work by repetition — say a small thing twice and it comes out meaning neither one nor the other.`}]},{index:4,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"so lala" with the tune — up, then down`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "so lala" with the tune — up, then down]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the ladder — "sehr gut", "gut", "so lala", "nicht so gut"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the ladder — "sehr gut", "gut", "so lala", "nicht so gut"]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the four shrugs — so lala, comme ci comme ça, más o menos, così così`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the four shrugs — *so lala*, *comme ci comme ça*, *más o menos*, *così così*]`}]},{index:5,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does so lala mean? ("So-so.") What does lala mean on its own? (Nothing — it is pure sing-song.) What do three of the four shrugs have in common? (They repeat a small word.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-es-geht`,sequence:130,title:`es geht — the German shrug: "it goes"`,headword:`es geht`,romanization:`es geht`,gloss:`"it goes" — the German shrug, and what saying nothing extra means`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:bc306b89f08c1a48`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Someone asks Wie geht's? You are neither great nor terrible. German has an answer for exactly that, and it is made of two words you already own.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: es geht`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`es geht — "it goes." becomes "So-so. I'm getting by."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Nothing has been added. That is the whole trick.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Es geht mir gut says it goes well. Take the gut away and German does not become vague — it becomes pointed. The listener was waiting for a gut or a schlecht, and you gave neither. The absence is the meaning.`},{kind:`speech`,text:`English does this too, with tone rather than deletion: "It's… going."`}]},{index:2,type:`grammar`,title:`Grammar Lens: the answer ladder`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`You now hold the whole range, top to bottom, with no word left unexplained:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Answer`,`Sense`],columns:2,rowCount:5,utterances:[`Sehr gut! means very well.`,`Gut, danke. means well, thanks.`,`Es geht. means so-so, getting by.`,`So lala. means so-so, sung with a shrug.`,`Nicht so gut. means not so well.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Es geht and so lala sit on the same rung and differ only in mood: es geht is dry and a little stoic, so lala is light and musical. Choose by the face you want to make.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"es geht" — flat, unhurried`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "es geht" — flat, unhurried]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"es geht mir gut", then just "es geht" — hear the gut go missing`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "es geht mir gut", then just "es geht" — hear the *gut* go missing]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the ladder top to bottom — "Sehr gut", "Gut danke", "Es geht", "So lala", "Nicht so gut"`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the ladder top to bottom — "Sehr gut", "Gut danke", "Es geht", "So lala", "Nicht so gut"]`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`What does es geht mean literally, and in use? ("It goes"; "so-so.") What makes it mean so-so? (The missing gut — the absence is the message.) Which answer sits at the top of the ladder? (Sehr gut.) At the bottom? (Nicht so gut.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-und`,sequence:132,title:`und — "and," and how to hand the question back`,headword:`und`,romanization:`und`,gloss:`and — and the two-word move that returns a question`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:2f2469e222ce4463`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Someone asks how you are; you answer. A conversation only starts when you ask back — and German returns the question in two words.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`You'll want to know: und`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`und — "and."`},{kind:`speech`,text:`It is English and with the vowel darkened, and it has barely changed in a thousand years — one of the oldest words either language still uses daily.`}]},{index:2,type:`pronunciation`,title:`Sounds you'll need`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`d-t-final — say oont, not oond. German hardens a d at the end of a word into a t. It is the same rule that makes Tag come out as tock rather than tahg: whatever the spelling, a final consonant goes voiceless. So und rhymes with English hunt, not with shunned.`}]},{index:3,type:`grammar`,title:`Grammar Lens: returning the question`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Hand the question back by naming the other person — in the to-whom form, because it is short for "and how goes it to you?"`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`To a friend`,`To a stranger`],columns:2,rowCount:1,utterances:[`To a friend: und dir? To a stranger: und Ihnen?`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Not und du — the dative is doing real work here, and using the doer-form is the giveaway of a beginner. The same shape appears across the series: Spanish ¿y tú?, French et toi?`}]},{index:4,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"und" (oont, hard t)`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "und" (*oont*, hard *t*)]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"und dir?" — and you, to a friend`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "und dir?" — and you, to a friend]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"und Ihnen?" — and you, to a stranger`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "und Ihnen?" — and you, to a stranger]`}]},{index:5,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`How is und pronounced, and why? (Oont — a final d hardens to t.) Return the question to a friend. (Und dir?) To a stranger? (Und Ihnen?) Why not und du? (It is short for "how goes it to you" — the to-whom form.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-practice`,sequence:135,title:`Practice — Wie geht's?, casually`,headword:`(dialogue)`,romanization:`(dialogue)`,gloss:`Chapter 3 recap — the casual wellbeing exchange`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:2a06688c929400ba`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`No new words. Everything below has had its own lesson; this is the first time they run together at speed.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`The exchange`,segments:[{kind:`table`,headers:[`German`,`English`],columns:2,rowCount:4,utterances:[`Hallo! Wie geht's? means Hi! How are you?`,`Gut, danke, und dir? means Well, thanks — and you?`,`Es geht. means So-so.`,`Danke! — Bitte. means Thanks! — You're welcome.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Four moves: greet, ask, answer-and-return, close the courtesy loop.`}]},{index:2,type:`grammar`,title:`Grammar Lens: why dir, and not du`,segments:[{kind:`speech`,text:`Und dir? keeps the dative because it is short for the full question — "and how goes it to you?" Saying und du would be the one wrong note in the whole exchange, and it is the commonest beginner slip there is.`}]},{index:3,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the whole exchange, both voices`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the whole exchange, both voices]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Wie geht's? — Gut, danke."`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Wie geht's? — Gut, danke."]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Und dir? — Es geht."`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Und dir? — Es geht."]`},{kind:`repeat`,times:2,source:`[REPEAT x2]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`"Wie geht's? — Gut, danke, und dir?"`}]},{index:4,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Run the casual exchange without looking. Which answer is the so-so one? (Es geht.) Which word answers danke? (Bitte.) Why dir and not du? (The question means "how goes it to you" — the to-whom form.)`}]}]},{id:`GE-C03-formal-practice`,sequence:140,title:`Formal practice — Wie geht es Ihnen?`,headword:`(dialogue)`,romanization:`(dialogue)`,gloss:`Chapter 3 payoff — the same exchange, formally`,script:`latin`,modality:`voice`,derivedModality:`voice`,modalityReasons:[`no-visual-dependency`],sourceHash:`fnv1a64:4c3690d705450a1b`,notice:null,blocks:[{index:0,type:`warmup`,title:`Warm-up`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:2,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 2s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Same exchange, same order, same verb. One word changes throughout — and that is the chapter's whole argument about German politeness.`}]},{index:1,type:`input`,title:`The exchange`,segments:[{kind:`table`,headers:[`German`,`English`],columns:2,rowCount:4,utterances:[`Guten Tag. Wie geht es Ihnen? means Good day. How are you?`,`Danke, gut. Und Ihnen? means Well, thanks. And you?`,`Es geht. means So-so.`,`Danke! — Bitte. means Thanks! — You're welcome.`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Set it beside the casual version and the difference is two words, both of them the listener:`},{kind:`table`,headers:[``,`Casual`,`Formal`],columns:3,rowCount:2,utterances:[`the question. Casual: Wie geht's? Formal: Wie geht es Ihnen?`,`the return. Casual: und dir? Formal: und Ihnen?`]},{kind:`speech`,text:`Danke, gut, es geht and bitte do not shift at all. German courtesy words are register-neutral; the politeness is carried entirely by the pronoun.`}]},{index:2,type:`guided-production`,title:`Guided Practice`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:1,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 1s]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`the formal exchange, both voices`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: the formal exchange, both voices]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Wie geht es Ihnen? — Danke, gut."`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Wie geht es Ihnen? — Danke, gut."]`},{kind:`prompt`,action:`SAY`,instruction:`"Und Ihnen? — Es geht."`,spoken:!0,scored:!1,responseSeconds:8,source:`[YOU SAY: "Und Ihnen? — Es geht."]`},{kind:`repeat`,times:2,source:`[REPEAT x2]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`"Wie geht es Ihnen? — Danke, gut. Und Ihnen?"`}]},{index:3,type:`recall`,title:`Wrap-up Recall`,segments:[{kind:`pause`,seconds:3,perItem:!1,source:`[PAUSE 3s]`},{kind:`speech`,text:`Which greeting opens the formal exchange? (Guten Tag.) Which dative pronoun appears twice? (Ihnen.) What changes from the casual version? (Dir becomes Ihnen — nothing else.) Where does German keep its politeness? (In the pronoun.)`}]}]}],findings:[]};export{e as default};